const SOLUTIONS = {
 "1": "<p>Multiply the two complex gains using the distributive property:<br>$$(2 + 3i)(4 - i) = 2(4) + 2(-i) + 3i(4) + 3i(-i) = 8 - 2i + 12i - 3i^2.$$<br>Since $i^2 = -1$, the last term is $-3(-1) = +3$:<br>$$8 + 10i + 3 = 11 + 10i.$$<br><strong>Final answer: the total gain is $11 + 10i$.</strong> The real part 11 and imaginary part 10 together describe the combined amplification and phase shift of the two stages.</p>",
 "2": "<p>(a) Expand using the distributive property (FOIL):<br>$$(x + 4i)^2 = x^2 + 4ix + 4ix + 16i^2 = x^2 + 8ix + 16(-1) = x^2 - 16 + 8ix.$$<br><strong>Final answer (a): $(x + 4i)^2 = x^2 - 16 + 8ix$.</strong></p>\n<p>(b) A sum of squares factors over the complex numbers using conjugates. Since $64 = 8^2$:<br>$$x^2 + 64 = (x + 8i)(x - 8i).$$<br>Check: $(x + 8i)(x - 8i) = x^2 - 8ix + 8ix - 64i^2 = x^2 - 64(-1) = x^2 + 64$. ✓<br><strong>Final answer (b): $x^2 + 64 = (x + 8i)(x - 8i)$.</strong> The filter polynomial has no real factors, but it splits completely once complex numbers are allowed.</p>",
 "3": "<p>Factor by grouping:<br>$$x^3 - x^2 + 4x - 4 = x^2(x - 1) + 4(x - 1) = (x - 1)(x^2 + 4).$$<br>Set each factor equal to zero:<br>• $x - 1 = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 1$.<br>• $x^2 + 4 = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2 = -4 \\Rightarrow x = 2i$ or $x = -2i$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the solutions are $x = 1$, $x = 2i$, and $x = -2i$.</strong> There are exactly 3 solutions counting multiplicity, matching the degree 3 of the polynomial, as the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra guarantees. In the design context, only $x = 1$ ft is a physically meaningful dimension; the two complex solutions are still required for a complete algebraic solution.</p>",
 "4": "<p>(a) The factor $p$ is the <strong>price charged per basket</strong>, in dollars. The factor $200 - 4p$ is the <strong>number of baskets sold per day</strong>: she would sell 200 baskets if they were free, and each \\$1 increase in price reduces the number sold. Their product is price × quantity, which is revenue.</p>\n<p>(b) In the expanded form $R(p) = 200p - 4p^2$, the coefficient $-4$ is the <strong>rate at which sales drop per dollar of price increase</strong>: each additional \\$1 in the price costs her 4 basket sales per day (this is also why the sales factor falls by 4 per unit of $p$).</p>\n<p>(c) Sales reach zero when $200 - 4p = 0$, i.e., $4p = 200$, so $p = 50$.</p>\n<p>**Final answer: at a price of \\$50 per basket she sells zero baskets** (and so earns \\$0 revenue) — the sales factor $200 - 4p$ is the part of the expression that reveals this.</p>",
 "5": "<p>By the Remainder Theorem, the remainder on division by $x - 4$ equals $P(4)$. Use synthetic substitution with $a = 4$ on the coefficients $1, -5, 2, 8$:</p>\n<ul><li>Bring down $1$; multiply by 4: $4$; add to $-5$: $-1$.</li><li>Multiply by 4: $-4$; add to $2$: $-2$.</li><li>Multiply by 4: $-8$; add to $8$: $0$.</li></ul>\n<p>The remainder is $0$. (Direct check: $P(4) = 64 - 5(16) + 2(4) + 8 = 64 - 80 + 8 + 8 = 0$. ✓)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the remainder is $0$, so $P(4) = 0$ hundred dollars \u000018$x - 4$19\u0000x - 4$ is a factor of $P(x)$.</strong> In context, the manufacturer exactly breaks even at month 4 — no profit and no loss that month.</p>",
 "6": "<p>(a) The zeros come from the factors: $x - 1 = 0$, $x - 4 = 0$, $x - 6 = 0$, so the zeros are $x = 1$, $x = 4$, and $x = 6$, each with multiplicity 1. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> the startup breaks even at the end of years 1, 4, and 6.</p>\n<p>(b) $P(0) = (0 - 1)(0 - 4)(0 - 6) = (-1)(-4)(-6) = -24$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> the $y$-intercept is $-24$, i.e., the startup starts with a loss of \\$24,000 at opening.</p>\n<p>(c) $P$ is a cubic with positive leading coefficient, so as $x \\to -\\infty$, $P(x) \\to -\\infty$, and as $x \\to \\infty$, $P(x) \\to \\infty$.</p>\n<p>(d) Sign analysis on $[0, 8]$ (each zero has multiplicity 1, so the sign changes at each zero):<br>• $0 \\le x &lt; 1$: negative (e.g., $P(0) = -24$) — loss.<br>• $1 &lt; x &lt; 4$: positive — profit.<br>• $4 &lt; x &lt; 6$: negative — loss.<br>• $6 &lt; x \\le 8$: positive — profit.</p>\n<p>The sketch crosses the $x$-axis at $(1, 0)$, $(4, 0)$, and $(6, 0)$, crosses the $y$-axis at $(0, -24)$, dips and rises through those sign intervals, and heads upward to the right.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: zeros at years 1, 4, and 6 (break-even points); starting loss of \\$24,000; the startup operates at a loss during $0 \\le x &lt; 1$ and during $4 &lt; x &lt; 6$, and is profitable during $1 &lt; x &lt; 4$ and $6 &lt; x \\le 8$.</strong></p>",
 "7": "<p>Divide $2x^2 + 7x + 10$ by $x + 3$ using polynomial long division:<br>• $2x^2 \\div x = 2x$. Multiply: $2x(x + 3) = 2x^2 + 6x$. Subtract: $(2x^2 + 7x) - (2x^2 + 6x) = x$. Bring down $10$.<br>• $x \\div x = 1$. Multiply: $1(x + 3) = x + 3$. Subtract: $10 - 3 = 7$.</p>\n<p>So the quotient is $2x + 1$ with remainder $7$:<br>$$L = 2x + 1 + \\frac{7}{x + 3}\\ \\text{feet}.$$<br>At $x = 4$: $L = 2(4) + 1 + \\dfrac{7}{4 + 3} = 8 + 1 + 1 = 10$ feet.<br>Check with the original fraction: $L = \\dfrac{2(16) + 7(4) + 10}{4 + 3} = \\dfrac{32 + 28 + 10}{7} = \\dfrac{70}{7} = 10$ feet. ✓</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: $L = 2x + 1 + \\dfrac{7}{x + 3}$ feet, and when $x = 4$ ft the patio is $10$ feet long</strong> (with width $7$ ft and area $70\\ \\text{ft}^2$, consistent with the given area formula).</p>",
 "8": "<p>(a) Use the common denominator $(x - 2)(x + 2) = x^2 - 4$:<br>$$\\frac{3}{x - 2} + \\frac{2}{x + 2} = \\frac{3(x + 2) + 2(x - 2)}{(x - 2)(x + 2)} = \\frac{3x + 6 + 2x - 4}{x^2 - 4} = \\frac{5x + 2}{x^2 - 4}, \\qquad x \\ne 2,\\ -2.$$</p>\n<p>(b) At $x = 6$: $\\dfrac{5(6) + 2}{36 - 4} = \\dfrac{32}{32} = 1$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the combined flow rate is $\\dfrac{5x + 2}{x^2 - 4}$ gallons per minute (for $x &gt; 2$), and at a setting of $x = 6$ the two valves together deliver $1$ gallon per minute.</strong></p>",
 "9": "<p>(a) Each pump&#x27;s rate is the reciprocal of its solo time, and the rates add:<br>$$\\frac{1}{x - 2} + \\frac{1}{x + 2} = \\frac{3}{8}.$$</p>\n<p>(b) Combine the left side over the denominator $x^2 - 4$:<br>$$\\frac{(x + 2) + (x - 2)}{x^2 - 4} = \\frac{2x}{x^2 - 4} = \\frac{3}{8}.$$<br>Cross-multiply: $16x = 3(x^2 - 4) = 3x^2 - 12$, so $3x^2 - 16x - 12 = 0$.<br>Factor: $3x^2 - 16x - 12 = (3x + 2)(x - 6) = 0$, giving $x = 6$ or $x = -\\tfrac{2}{3}$.</p>\n<p>Check $x = 6$: $\\tfrac{1}{4} + \\tfrac{1}{8} = \\tfrac{3}{8}$. ✓<br>The solution $x = -\\tfrac{2}{3}$ would give solo times of $x - 2 = -\\tfrac{8}{3}$ hours and $x + 2 = \\tfrac{4}{3}$ hours; a negative draining time is physically impossible, so it is rejected.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Pump 1 alone drains the basement in $6 - 2 = 4$ hours, and Pump 2 alone takes $6 + 2 = 8$ hours.</strong></p>",
 "10": "<p>Set the models equal and solve:<br>$$\\sqrt{x + 7} = x - 5.$$<br>Square both sides: $x + 7 = (x - 5)^2 = x^2 - 10x + 25$, so $x^2 - 11x + 18 = 0$.<br>Factor: $(x - 9)(x - 2) = 0$, giving candidates $x = 9$ and $x = 2$.</p>\n<p>Check each candidate in the <strong>original</strong> equation:<br>• $x = 9$: $\\sqrt{9 + 7} = \\sqrt{16} = 4$ and $9 - 5 = 4$. ✓ The models agree.<br>• $x = 2$: $\\sqrt{2 + 7} = \\sqrt{9} = 3$ but $2 - 5 = -3$. ✗ $3 \\ne -3$.</p>\n<p>The candidate $x = 2$ is extraneous: squaring turned the equation into one that is also satisfied when the two sides are opposites ($\\sqrt{x+7} = -(x-5)$), and a square root can never equal the negative value $-3$.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the two models agree only at $x = 9$, where both predict a clearance of $4$ feet; $x = 2$ is an extraneous solution introduced by squaring.</strong></p>",
 "11": "<p>(a) Plan A: $C = 3x + 40$. Plan B: $C = 6x + 10$. Graphed on the same axes ($x$ = classes, $C$ = total cost in dollars), Plan A is the line with $y$-intercept 40 and slope 3; Plan B is the line with $y$-intercept 10 and slope 6. Plan B starts lower but rises faster, so the lines cross.</p>\n<p>(b) At the intersection, both plans give the same cost, so set the expressions equal:<br>$$3x + 40 = 6x + 10 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 30 = 3x \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x = 10,$$<br>and the cost there is $C = 3(10) + 40 = \\$70$ (check Plan B: $6(10) + 10 = \\$70$ ✓).<br>The $x$-coordinate of the intersection is the solution of $f(x) = g(x)$ because an intersection point lies on <strong>both</strong> graphs: its coordinates satisfy both cost equations at once, which is exactly the statement that the two costs are equal for that number of classes.</p>\n<p>(c) For $x &lt; 10$ Plan B is cheaper (e.g., at $x = 0$: \\$10 vs. \\$40). For $x &gt; 10$ Plan A is cheaper (e.g., at $x = 20$: \\$100 vs. \\$130).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the plans cost the same — \\$70 — at $10$ classes; Plan B is the better deal for fewer than 10 classes, and Plan A is the better deal for more than 10 classes.</strong></p>",
 "12": "<p>(a) Start with $V = \\tfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h$. Multiply both sides by 3: $3V = \\pi r^2 h$. Divide both sides by $\\pi r^2$:<br>$$h = \\frac{3V}{\\pi r^2}.$$</p>\n<p>(b) Substitute $V = 37.68\\ \\text{m}^3$, $r = 3$ m, $\\pi \\approx 3.14$:<br>$$h = \\frac{3(37.68)}{3.14 \\cdot (3)^2} = \\frac{113.04}{3.14 \\cdot 9} = \\frac{113.04}{28.26} = 4\\ \\text{m}.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the gravel pile is $4$ meters tall.</strong> (Check: $\\tfrac{1}{3}(3.14)(9)(4) = 37.68\\ \\text{m}^3$. ✓)</p>",
 "13": "<p>Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, $1 + 2i$:<br>$$I = \\frac{10 - 5i}{1 - 2i} \\cdot \\frac{1 + 2i}{1 + 2i}.$$<br>Numerator: $(10 - 5i)(1 + 2i) = 10 + 20i - 5i - 10i^2 = 10 + 15i - 10(-1) = 20 + 15i$.<br>Denominator: $(1 - 2i)(1 + 2i) = 1 - (2i)^2 = 1 - 4i^2 = 1 + 4 = 5$.<br>$$I = \\frac{20 + 15i}{5} = 4 + 3i.$$</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the current is $4 + 3i$ amps.</strong> The conjugate turned the denominator into the real number 5, so the current can be read in standard $a + bi$ form.</p>",
 "14": "<p>(a) Rewrite the division as multiplication by the reciprocal, then factor everything:<br>$$\\frac{6x + 12}{x - 3} \\div \\frac{3x + 6}{x^2 - 9} = \\frac{6x + 12}{x - 3} \\cdot \\frac{x^2 - 9}{3x + 6} = \\frac{6(x + 2)}{x - 3} \\cdot \\frac{(x - 3)(x + 3)}{3(x + 2)}.$$<br>Cancel the common factors $(x + 2)$, $(x - 3)$, and a factor of 3:<br>$$= 2(x + 3) = 2x + 6.$$</p>\n<p>(b) Excluded values come from every denominator and from the divisor&#x27;s numerator: $x - 3 \\ne 0$ gives $x \\ne 3$; $x^2 - 9 \\ne 0$ gives $x \\ne 3, -3$; $3x + 6 \\ne 0$ gives $x \\ne -2$. <strong>Excluded: $x = 3$, $x = -3$, and $x = -2$.</strong></p>\n<p>(c) At $x = 4$: $2(4) + 6 = 14$. (Check in the original: $\\tfrac{36}{1} \\div \\tfrac{18}{7} = 36 \\cdot \\tfrac{7}{18} = 14$. ✓)</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the ratio simplifies to $2x + 6$ (valid for $x \\ne 3, -3, -2$), and at a machine setting of $x = 4$ the output ratio is $14$.</strong></p>",
 "15": "<p>(a) The equation is a polynomial equation of degree 4, so by the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra it has exactly <strong>4 complex solutions, counting multiplicity</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) Substitute $u = x^2$:<br>$$u^2 + 5u + 4 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; (u + 1)(u + 4) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; u = -1 \\text{ or } u = -4.$$<br>• $x^2 = -1 \\Rightarrow x = i$ or $x = -i$ (equivalently, $x^2 + 1 = (x + i)(x - i)$).<br>• $x^2 = -4 \\Rightarrow x = 2i$ or $x = -2i$ (equivalently, $x^2 + 4 = (x + 2i)(x - 2i)$).</p>\n<p>Indeed, $x^4 + 5x^2 + 4 = (x^2 + 1)(x^2 + 4) = (x + i)(x - i)(x + 2i)(x - 2i)$.</p>\n<p>(c) None of the four solutions is a real number. A real solution of the equation would be an $x$-intercept of the graph; since there are none, the graph of $y = x^4 + 5x^2 + 4$ never touches or crosses the $x$-axis (consistent with $x^4 + 5x^2 + 4 \\ge 4 &gt; 0$ for all real $x$).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: the four solutions are $x = i$, $x = -i$, $x = 2i$, and $x = -2i$ — four complex solutions as the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra requires, none of them real, so the associated graph has no $x$-intercepts.</strong></p>",
 "16": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> The flight lasts from launch ($t = 0$) to recovery ($t = 60$), so the <strong>domain is $[0, 60]$ minutes</strong>. The altitude rises from 200 ft to a peak of 2,600 ft and returns to 200 ft, so the <strong>range is $[200, 2600]$ feet</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> On the first segment the balloon climbs at a rate of $\\dfrac{2600 - 200}{24 - 0} = \\dfrac{2400}{24} = 100$ ft/min. So<br>$$h(12) = 200 + 100(12) = 200 + 1200 = 1400 \\text{ ft}.$$<br><strong>Interpretation:</strong> 12 minutes after launch, the balloon is 1,400 feet above the ground.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> <em>Going up:</em> from part (b), $h(12) = 1400$, so the balloon first reaches 1,400 ft at <strong>$t = 12$ min</strong>.<br><em>Coming down:</em> the second segment falls at a rate of $\\dfrac{200 - 2600}{60 - 24} = \\dfrac{-2400}{36} = -\\dfrac{200}{3} \\approx -66.7$ ft/min. Solve:<br>$$2600 - \\tfrac{200}{3}(t - 24) = 1400 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\tfrac{200}{3}(t-24) = 1200 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; t - 24 = 18 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; t = 42 \\text{ min}.$$<br><strong>Interpretation:</strong> The balloon is at 1,400 feet twice — once at 12 minutes on the way up and once at 42 minutes on the way down — because the flight path rises to a peak and then descends through the same altitude.</p>",
 "17": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> $P(60) = 45(60) - 1200 = 2700 - 1200 = 1500$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> With 60 members enrolled, the fitness center makes a monthly profit of <strong>$1,500</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Set the profit equal to the goal and solve:<br>$$45m - 1200 = 2400 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 45m = 3600 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; m = 80.$$<br><strong>Interpretation:</strong> The center needs <strong>80 members</strong> to reach a monthly profit of exactly $2,400.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Break-even: $45m - 1200 = 0 \\Rightarrow 45m = 1200 \\Rightarrow m = \\dfrac{1200}{45} \\approx 26.7$. Since memberships come in whole numbers, 26 members still leaves the center slightly in the red, so the center needs <strong>at least 27 members</strong> to avoid losing money.</p>",
 "18": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> At $t = 0$ the height is <strong>4 inches</strong>. This is the initial value of the function — the $H$-intercept of its graph.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> $H$ is <strong>increasing on $[0, 20]$</strong> (the tank is filling), <strong>constant on $[20, 30]$</strong> (height stays at 16 inches — a plausible explanation is that the crew paused filling, or inflow and outflow were balanced, e.g., while a valve was checked), and <strong>decreasing on $[30, 60]$</strong> (the tank is being drained).</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Average rate of change on $[0,20]$:<br>$$\\frac{H(20) - H(0)}{20 - 0} = \\frac{16 - 4}{20} = \\frac{12}{20} = 0.6 \\text{ in/min}.$$<br><strong>Interpretation:</strong> During the first 20 minutes, the water level rose at an average rate of <strong>0.6 inches per minute</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> During draining, the height falls from 12 in at $t = 40$ to 8 in at $t = 50$, a rate of $\\dfrac{8-12}{10} = -0.4$ in/min. Starting from 12 in, the height must drop 2 in to reach 10 in: $\\dfrac{2}{0.4} = 5$ minutes. So the height is 10 inches at about <strong>$t = 45$ minutes</strong>.</p>",
 "19": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> Appropriate domain: <strong>$n \\in \\{0, 1, 2, \\dots, 150\\}$</strong> — the whole numbers from 0 to 150. Two reasons it is not all real numbers: (1) passengers are counted in whole numbers, so fractional or negative passenger counts are meaningless; (2) the ferry&#x27;s capacity caps the input at 150 passengers.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Constraint: $18n \\geq 1800 \\Rightarrow n \\geq 100$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The company needs <strong>at least 100 passengers</strong> on board for the trip to run.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> The claim confuses algebra with modeling. $R(75.5) = 18(75.5) = \\$1{,}359$ can be computed, but a ferry cannot carry half a passenger; inputs to this model must be whole numbers, so $n = 75.5$ is not a viable option in the situation even though the arithmetic is defined.</p>",
 "20": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> The piecewise definition is:<br>$$C(h) = \\begin{cases} 50, &amp; 0 &lt; h \\leq 2 \\\\ 50 + 15(h - 2), &amp; 2 &lt; h \\leq 8 \\\\ 140, &amp; 8 &lt; h \\leq 12 \\end{cases}$$</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> The graph is horizontal at $C = 50$ from $h = 0$ to $h = 2$; then rises with slope 15 from $(2, 50)$ to $(8, 140)$; then is horizontal at the cap value $C = 140$ from $h = 8$ to $h = 12$. Breakpoints: $(2, 50)$ and $(8, 140)$, with the function continuous at both.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> $C(5) = 50 + 15(5 - 2) = 50 + 45 = 95$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> A 5-hour rental costs <strong>$95</strong>.<br>$C(1.5) = 50$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> A 1.5-hour rental costs <strong>$50</strong>, since it falls within the flat-fee first two hours.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> The cap begins when the middle piece reaches 140:<br>$$50 + 15(h - 2) = 140 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 15(h-2) = 90 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; h - 2 = 6 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; h = 8.$$<br><strong>Interpretation:</strong> Starting at a rental length of <strong>8 hours</strong>, the charge stops increasing and stays at the $140 cap.</p>",
 "21": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> The zeros come from setting each factor to zero: $x = 0$, $24 - 2x = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 12$, and $18 - 2x = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 9$. The graph crosses the $x$-axis at <strong>$x = 0$, $x = 9$, and $x = 12$</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Expanding, $V(x) = x(24 - 2x)(18 - 2x) = 4x^3 - 84x^2 + 432x$. The leading term is $4x^3$ with a <strong>positive</strong> leading coefficient, so as $x \\to -\\infty$, $V(x) \\to -\\infty$, and as $x \\to \\infty$, $V(x) \\to \\infty$ — the standard end behavior of a cubic with positive leading coefficient.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> In context, the cut size must be positive, and the shorter side of the cardboard (18 in) limits the cut: cutting $x$ from both ends requires $18 - 2x &gt; 0$, i.e., $x &lt; 9$. The contextual domain is <strong>$0 &lt; x &lt; 9$ inches</strong>. The zeros $x = 0$ (no cut, no box) and $x = 9$ and $x = 12$ (the cardboard is completely used up or over-cut, giving zero or negative dimensions) are excluded or lie outside this domain.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> Evaluating near the peak: $V(3) = 3(18)(12) = 648$ in³; $V(3.5) = 3.5(17)(11) = 654.5$ in³; $V(4) = 4(16)(10) = 640$ in³. The graph/table shows the maximum occurs at about <strong>$x \\approx 3.5$ inches</strong>, giving a maximum volume of about <strong>655 cubic inches</strong>.</p>",
 "22": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> The graph starts at the $C$-intercept <strong>$(0, 180)$</strong> and decreases toward the $t$-axis, getting flatter as $t$ grows. It has <strong>no $t$-intercepts</strong> (the curve never reaches $C = 0$ in finite time). The horizontal asymptote is <strong>$C = 0$</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> The $C$-intercept means the bloodstream initially contains <strong>180 mg</strong> of caffeine, the amount in the cup of coffee. The asymptote $C = 0$ means the caffeine level decreases toward zero over time but, in the model, never quite reaches it.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> $C(5) = 180(0.87)^5$. Since $(0.87)^5 \\approx 0.4984$, $C(5) \\approx 180(0.4984) \\approx 89.7$ mg. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> Five hours after the coffee, about <strong>90 mg</strong> of caffeine — roughly half the original amount — remains in the bloodstream.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> Solve $180(0.87)^t = 20$:<br>$$(0.87)^t = \\frac{20}{180} = \\frac{1}{9} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; t = \\frac{\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)}{\\log(0.87)} = \\frac{-0.9542}{-0.06048} \\approx 15.8.$$<br><strong>Interpretation:</strong> The caffeine level drops to 20 mg after approximately <strong>15.8 hours</strong>.</p>",
 "23": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> From the table, at $m = 0$ the cost is $50, so Company B&#x27;s base fee is **$50**. The cost rises by $20 for every 100 miles, a rate of $\\dfrac{20}{100} = \\$0.20$ per mile. So $C_B(m) = 50 + 0.20m$.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Base fees: Company A charges $38, Company B charges $50 — <strong>Company A has the lower base fee</strong>. Per-mile rates: Company A charges $0.25/mile, Company B charges $0.20/mile — <strong>Company B has the lower per-mile rate</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Set the costs equal:<br>$$38 + 0.25m = 50 + 0.20m \\;\\Rightarrow\\; 0.05m = 12 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; m = 240.$$<br>Cost at that mileage: $C_A(240) = 38 + 0.25(240) = 38 + 60 = \\$98$ (check: $C_B(240) = 50 + 0.20(240) = 50 + 48 = \\$98$ ✓). <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The companies cost the same — <strong>$98 — at 240 miles</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> At 300 miles: $C_A(300) = 38 + 0.25(300) = 38 + 75 = \\$113$; $C_B(300) = 50 + 0.20(300) = 50 + 60 = \\$110$. <strong>Recommendation:</strong> Choose <strong>Company B</strong>; at 300 miles (beyond the 240-mile break-even point) it costs <strong>$110 versus $113, saving $3</strong>.</p>",
 "24": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> Recursive definition: $a_1 = 12$ and $a_n = a_{n-1} + 1.5$ for $n \\geq 2$.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Explicit formula: $a_n = 12 + 1.5(n - 1)$, which simplifies to $a_n = 1.5n + 10.5$.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> $a_{10} = 12 + 1.5(9) = 12 + 13.5 = 25.5$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> In week 10 she runs <strong>25.5 miles</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> Solve $12 + 1.5(n - 1) = 30$: $1.5(n-1) = 18 \\Rightarrow n - 1 = 12 \\Rightarrow n = 13$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> Her weekly mileage first reaches 30 miles in <strong>week 13</strong>.</p>",
 "25": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> Cost: $C(x) = 250 + 3.25x$ dollars. Revenue: $R(x) = 9.50x$ dollars.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Profit is revenue minus cost, so use subtraction:<br>$$P(x) = R(x) - C(x) = 9.50x - (250 + 3.25x) = 6.25x - 250.$$</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Break-even: $6.25x - 250 = 0 \\Rightarrow 6.25x = 250 \\Rightarrow x = 40$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The truck must sell <strong>40 sandwiches</strong> in a day to exactly cover its costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> $P(120) = 6.25(120) - 250 = 750 - 250 = 500$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> Selling 120 sandwiches in a day yields a profit of <strong>$500</strong>.</p>",
 "26": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> Starting from $f(x) = x^2$, the transformations are: <strong>reflect across the $x$-axis</strong> (the negative sign), <strong>vertical compression by a factor of 0.008</strong> (the coefficient), <strong>shift right 50 units</strong> (the $x - 50$), and <strong>shift up 20 units</strong> (the $+20$). Together: $A(x) = -0.008\\, f(x - 50) + 20$.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> The vertex is <strong>$(50, 20)$</strong>. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The arch reaches its maximum height of <strong>20 feet</strong> at the midpoint of the bridge, 50 feet from the near bank.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Solve $-0.008(x - 50)^2 + 20 = 0$:<br>$$(x - 50)^2 = \\frac{20}{0.008} = 2500 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x - 50 = \\pm 50 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x = 0 \\text{ or } x = 100.$$<br><strong>Interpretation:</strong> The arch meets the water at $x = 0$ ft and $x = 100$ ft — exactly at the two banks of the 100-foot-wide river.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> $A(25) = -0.008(25 - 50)^2 + 20 = -0.008(625) + 20 = -5 + 20 = 15$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> At a point 25 feet from the near bank, the arch is <strong>15 feet</strong> above the water.</p>\n<p><strong>(e)</strong> The graph of $A$ is symmetric about the vertical line <strong>$x = 50$</strong> (the line through the vertex), because points equally far left and right of mid-span have equal heights. A function is <strong>even</strong> only if it is symmetric about the $y$-axis, i.e., $A(-x) = A(x)$ for all $x$. Here $A(-25) = -0.008(5625) + 20 = -25 \\neq A(25) = 15$, so $A$ is not even — its symmetry axis is $x = 50$, not $x = 0$.</p>",
 "27": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> To invert the function, solve the equation $C = 65h + 85$ for $h$ (equivalently, swap the roles of the variables and solve):<br>$$C = 65h + 85 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; C - 85 = 65h \\;\\Rightarrow\\; h = \\frac{C - 85}{65}.$$<br>So the inverse function is $\\;h(C) = \\dfrac{C - 85}{65}\\;$.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> In context, hours satisfy $h \\geq 0$, so for $C$: <strong>domain $h \\geq 0$, range $C \\geq 85$</strong> (the charge is at least the $85 base fee). For the inverse the roles swap: **domain $C \\geq 85$, range $h \\geq 0$**.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> $h(410) = \\dfrac{410 - 85}{65} = \\dfrac{325}{65} = 5$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> A $410 bill corresponds to <strong>5 hours</strong> of labor.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> $h(280) = \\dfrac{280 - 85}{65} = \\dfrac{195}{65} = 3$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> A $280 bill corresponds to **3 hours** of labor. Verification: $C(3) = 65(3) + 85 = 195 + 85 = 280$ ✓ — the original function returns the $280 charge.</p>",
 "28": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> <br>$$f(g(x)) = \\frac{5}{9}\\left(\\frac{9}{5}x + 32 - 32\\right) = \\frac{5}{9} \\cdot \\frac{9}{5}x = x.$$</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> <br>$$g(f(x)) = \\frac{9}{5}\\left(\\frac{5}{9}(x - 32)\\right) + 32 = (x - 32) + 32 = x.$$</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> Since $f(g(x)) = x$ and $g(f(x)) = x$, <strong>$f$ and $g$ are inverse functions</strong>. Physically, this means converting a temperature from Celsius to Fahrenheit and then back to Celsius (or the reverse) returns exactly the original temperature — the two conversions undo each other.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> $f(98.6) = \\frac{5}{9}(98.6 - 32) = \\frac{5}{9}(66.6) = \\frac{333}{9} = 37$. So $98.6^\\circ\\text{F} = \\mathbf{37^\\circ C}$. Round trip: $g(37) = \\frac{9}{5}(37) + 32 = 66.6 + 32 = 98.6$ ✓ — back to the original $98.6^\\circ\\text{F}$.</p>",
 "29": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> $f(15) = 1.9$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> After 15 minutes, the runner is <strong>1.9 miles</strong> from the start of the trail.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> $f^{-1}(2.6) = 20$. <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The runner reaches a distance of <strong>2.6 miles</strong> from the start after <strong>20 minutes</strong>. Compared with part (a), the roles have switched: for $f$ the input is time and the output is distance, while for $f^{-1}$ the input is distance (2.6 mi) and the output is the time (20 min) at which that distance is reached.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> The output 1.0 mile falls between $f(5) = 0.6$ and $f(10) = 1.3$. The fraction of the way is $\\dfrac{1.0 - 0.6}{1.3 - 0.6} = \\dfrac{0.4}{0.7} \\approx 0.57$, so $t \\approx 5 + 0.57(5) \\approx 7.9$. <strong>Estimate:</strong> $f^{-1}(1.0) \\approx$ <strong>8 minutes</strong> — the runner reaches the 1-mile mark after roughly 8 minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> The domain of $f^{-1}$ is the range of $f$: <strong>$[0, 3.2]$ miles</strong>. The range of $f^{-1}$ is the domain of $f$: <strong>$[0, 25]$ minutes</strong>.</p>",
 "30": "<p><strong>(a)</strong> Table of values:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>$t$ (years)</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th></tr>\n<tr><td>$P(t) = t^2 + 20t + 100$</td><td>100</td><td>121</td><td>144</td><td>169</td><td>196</td><td>225</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$E(t) = 50(1.5)^t$</td><td>50</td><td>75</td><td>112.50</td><td>168.75</td><td>253.13</td><td>379.69</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>(Sample computations: $P(3) = 9 + 60 + 100 = 169$; $E(3) = 50(3.375) = 168.75$; $E(5) = 50(7.59375) \\approx 379.69$.)</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> Account P is ahead for <strong>$t = 0, 1, 2,$ and $3$</strong> (e.g., $100 &gt; 50$ at $t = 0$; $169 &gt; 168.75$ at $t = 3$). Account E first moves ahead at <strong>$t = 4$</strong>, when $E(4) \\approx \\$253.13$ versus $P(4) = \\$196$ — so the crossover happens between years 3 and 4.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> At $t = 3$ Account P leads by only $169 - 168.75 = \\$0.25$ — its early head start ($100 vs. $50) has been almost entirely erased because Account E grows by a constant *factor* (×1.5 each year) while Account P grows by smaller and smaller *relative* amounts. A lead that shrinks to 25 cents is about to disappear, and by $t = 4$ E is ahead by more than $57.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> <strong>Principle:</strong> A quantity increasing exponentially will eventually exceed a quantity increasing as a polynomial function, no matter how far behind it starts. Evidence from the table: at $t = 4$ the gap is $253.13 - 196 = \\$57.13$ in E&#x27;s favor, and just one year later at $t = 5$ the gap has grown to $379.69 - 225 = \\$154.69$ — the exponential account is not only ahead, it is pulling away at an accelerating rate, while Account P adds only about $29 per year.</p>",
 "31": "<p>(a) Convert radians to degrees:<br>$$\\frac{5\\pi}{3} \\text{ rad} \\times \\frac{180^\\circ}{\\pi \\text{ rad}} = 5 \\times 60^\\circ = 300^\\circ$$</p>\n<p>(b) Arc length is $s = r\\theta$, with $\\theta$ in radians:<br>$$s = 4 \\times \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{20\\pi}{3} \\approx 20.94 \\text{ m}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The rotation is $300^\\circ$, and the horse travels $\\frac{20\\pi}{3} \\approx 20.94$ meters along its circular path.</p>",
 "32": "<p>(a) Subtract full rotations: $750^\\circ - 2(360^\\circ) = 750^\\circ - 720^\\circ = 30^\\circ$. Because trigonometric functions are periodic with period $360^\\circ$ (one full turn returns the blade to the same direction), rotating $750^\\circ$ ends in exactly the same direction as rotating $30^\\circ$.</p>\n<p>(b) On the unit circle, the point at $30^\\circ$ is in Quadrant I, where both coordinates are positive:<br>$$\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\approx 0.866, \\qquad \\sin 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}$$<br>Scale by the blade length, 12 m:<br>$$x = 12\\cos 30^\\circ = 12 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 6\\sqrt{3} \\approx 10.39 \\text{ m}, \\qquad y = 12\\sin 30^\\circ = 12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 6 \\text{ m}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The blade points in the $30^\\circ$ direction, and its tip is at approximately $(10.39, 6.00)$ meters relative to the hub — about 10.39 m east and 6 m north of the hub.</p>",
 "33": "<p>(a) Amplitude $= \\dfrac{5.2 - 1.0}{2} = 2.1$ ft. Midline $= \\dfrac{5.2 + 1.0}{2} = 3.1$ ft. Period $= 12$ h, so the angular frequency is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ rad/h. Since the tide starts at its maximum at $t = 0$, use cosine with no phase shift:<br>$$h(t) = 3.1 + 2.1\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}t\\right) \\text{ feet}$$</p>\n<p>(b) At $t = 4$: $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\cdot 4\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{2}$, so<br>$$h(4) = 3.1 + 2.1\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 3.1 - 1.05 = 2.05 \\text{ ft}$$</p>\n<p>(c) The height equals the midline when $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}t\\right) = 0$, first at $\\frac{\\pi}{6}t = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, i.e., $t = 3$ hours. This is between high tide ($t = 0$) and low tide ($t = 6$), when the height is decreasing from 5.2 ft toward 1.0 ft, so the tide is falling.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> $h(t) = 3.1 + 2.1\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}t\\right)$ ft (amplitude 2.1 ft, midline 3.1 ft, period 12 h); the height 4 hours after high tide is 2.05 feet, and the club should launch at $t = 3$ hours after high tide.</p>",
 "34": "<p>The center of the wheel is $2 + 10 = 12$ m above the ground, so the midline is 12 m and the amplitude is 10 m (half the diameter). The period is 40 s, giving angular frequency $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{40} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{20}$ rad/s. The rider starts at the minimum height, so use a reflected cosine:</p>\n<p>(a) $$h(t) = 12 - 10\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{20}t\\right) \\text{ meters}$$</p>\n<p>(b) At $t = 10$: $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$, so $h(10) = 12 - 0 = 12$ m (level with the center, a quarter-turn up).<br>At $t = 20$: $\\cos(\\pi) = -1$, so $h(20) = 12 - 10(-1) = 22$ m (the top of the wheel: $2 + 20 = 22$ m ✓).</p>\n<p>(c) At $t = 5$: $\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\approx 0.7071$, so<br>$$h(5) = 12 - 10(0.7071) \\approx 12 - 7.07 = 4.93 \\text{ m}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> $h(t) = 12 - 10\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{20}t\\right)$ m; the rider is 12 m high at 10 s, 22 m high (the top) at 20 s, and about 4.93 m high at 5 s.</p>",
 "35": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> $A(0,0)$, $B(6,8)$, $C(12,0)$; $D$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{AB}$ and $E$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{AC}$. <strong>Prove:</strong> $\\overline{DE} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$ and $DE = \\frac{1}{2}BC$.</p>\n<p><em>Step 1 — Midpoints (Midpoint Formula).</em><br>$$D = \\left(\\frac{0+6}{2}, \\frac{0+8}{2}\\right) = (3,4), \\qquad E = \\left(\\frac{0+12}{2}, \\frac{0+0}{2}\\right) = (6,0)$$</p>\n<p><em>Step 2 — Parallel (Slope Formula).</em><br>$$\\text{slope of } \\overline{DE} = \\frac{0-4}{6-3} = -\\frac{4}{3}, \\qquad \\text{slope of } \\overline{BC} = \\frac{0-8}{12-6} = -\\frac{8}{6} = -\\frac{4}{3}$$<br>The slopes are equal, and two distinct lines with equal slopes are parallel, so $\\overline{DE} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$.</p>\n<p><em>Step 3 — Length relationship (Distance Formula).</em><br>$$DE = \\sqrt{(6-3)^2 + (0-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5 \\text{ m}$$<br>$$BC = \\sqrt{(12-6)^2 + (0-8)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100} = 10 \\text{ m}$$<br>Since $5 = \\frac{1}{2}(10)$, we have $DE = \\frac{1}{2}BC$. $\\blacksquare$</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Both claims are verified: the irrigation line $\\overline{DE}$ is parallel to $\\overline{BC}$, and its length (5 m) is exactly half of $BC$ (10 m), as the Triangle Midsegment Theorem requires.</p>",
 "36": "<p><strong>Given:</strong> Triangle $ABC$; line $DE$ through $A$ with $DE \\parallel BC$ and $D$–$A$–$E$ collinear. <strong>Prove:</strong> $m\\angle BAC + m\\angle B + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ$.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Statements</th><th>Reasons</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. $DE \\parallel BC$</td><td>1. Given (drawn in the diagram)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. $\\angle DAB \\cong \\angle B$, so $m\\angle DAB = m\\angle B$</td><td>2. Alternate Interior Angles Theorem (parallel lines $DE$ and $BC$ cut by transversal $AB$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. $\\angle EAC \\cong \\angle C$, so $m\\angle EAC = m\\angle C$</td><td>3. Alternate Interior Angles Theorem (parallel lines cut by transversal $AC$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. $m\\angle DAB + m\\angle BAC + m\\angle CAE = 180^\\circ$</td><td>4. $D$–$A$–$E$ form a straight line, so $\\angle DAE$ is a straight angle (Linear Pair / Angle Addition Postulate)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. $m\\angle B + m\\angle BAC + m\\angle C = 180^\\circ$</td><td>5. Substitution of Steps 2 and 3 into Step 4</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>$\\blacksquare$</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The interior angles of the triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, confirming the design software&#x27;s angle calculations rest on a valid theorem.</p>",
 "37": "<p>(a) <strong>Claim:</strong> If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then the quadrilateral is a parallelogram (converse of the Parallelogram Diagonals Theorem).</p>\n<p>Midpoint of diagonal $\\overline{WY}$:<br>$$\\left(\\frac{1+9}{2}, \\frac{1+6}{2}\\right) = (5, 3.5)$$<br>Midpoint of diagonal $\\overline{XZ}$:<br>$$\\left(\\frac{7+3}{2}, \\frac{2+5}{2}\\right) = (5, 3.5)$$<br>The two diagonals have the same midpoint, so each diagonal is split into two equal parts by the other — the diagonals bisect each other. By the converse theorem stated above, $WXYZ$ is a parallelogram. $\\blacksquare$</p>\n<p>(b) Side lengths (Distance Formula), in feet:<br>$$WX = \\sqrt{(7-1)^2 + (2-1)^2} = \\sqrt{36+1} = \\sqrt{37} \\approx 6.08 \\text{ ft}$$<br>$$XY = \\sqrt{(9-7)^2 + (6-2)^2} = \\sqrt{4+16} = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\approx 4.47 \\text{ ft}$$<br>$$YZ = \\sqrt{(3-9)^2 + (5-6)^2} = \\sqrt{36+1} = \\sqrt{37} \\approx 6.08 \\text{ ft}$$<br>$$ZW = \\sqrt{(1-3)^2 + (1-5)^2} = \\sqrt{4+16} = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5} \\approx 4.47 \\text{ ft}$$<br>$WX = YZ$ and $XY = ZW$, so opposite sides are congruent, consistent with a parallelogram.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> $WXYZ$ is a parallelogram — its diagonals both have midpoint $(5, 3.5)$, so they bisect each other — and the side check ($\\sqrt{37}$, $2\\sqrt{5}$, $\\sqrt{37}$, $2\\sqrt{5}$ ft) confirms it, so the platform panels will fit.</p>",
 "38": "<p>(a) The angle $\\angle ACB$ is an inscribed angle intercepting arc $AB$. The central angle $\\angle AOB = 124^\\circ$ intercepts the same (minor) arc $AB$, so the minor arc $AB$ measures $124^\\circ$. By the Inscribed Angle Theorem, an inscribed angle is half the measure of its intercepted arc:<br>$$m\\angle ACB = \\frac{1}{2}(124^\\circ) = 62^\\circ$$</p>\n<p>(b) If the spectator sat on the minor arc, the inscribed angle would intercept the <em>major</em> arc $AB$, which measures $360^\\circ - 124^\\circ = 236^\\circ$. The inscribed angle would then be $\\frac{1}{2}(236^\\circ) = 118^\\circ$ — a much wider viewing angle, because the intercepted arc is the rest of the circle.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The spectator&#x27;s viewing angle from the major arc is $62^\\circ$; from the minor arc it would be $118^\\circ$ instead, since the angle always measures half of whichever arc lies opposite the spectator.</p>",
 "39": "<p>(a) Draw radii $\\overline{OA}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ to the endpoints of the chord. This forms two right triangles, $\\triangle OMA$ and $\\triangle OMB$, with right angles at $M$ (since $\\overline{OM} \\perp \\overline{AB}$). The hypotenuses $\\overline{OA}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are congruent because all radii of a circle are congruent, and the leg $\\overline{OM}$ is common to both triangles. By the Hypotenuse–Leg Congruence Theorem, $\\triangle OMA \\cong \\triangle OMB$. Corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent, so $AM = MB$; that is, $M$ is the midpoint of the chord. $\\blacksquare$</p>\n<p>(b) From part (a), $AM = \\frac{1}{2}(16) = 8$ ft. In right $\\triangle OMA$, the hypotenuse is the radius, $OA = 10$ ft. By the Pythagorean Theorem:<br>$$OM = \\sqrt{OA^2 - AM^2} = \\sqrt{10^2 - 8^2} = \\sqrt{100 - 64} = \\sqrt{36} = 6 \\text{ ft}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The perpendicular radius bisects the 16-ft chord into two 8-ft halves, and the brace is 6 feet from the center of the culvert.</p>",
 "40": "<p>(a) Opposite angles of an inscribed quadrilateral are supplementary (their intercepted arcs together make the whole circle, $360^\\circ$, and each angle is half its arc). Therefore:<br>$$m\\angle C = 180^\\circ - m\\angle A = 180^\\circ - 85^\\circ = 95^\\circ$$<br>$$m\\angle D = 180^\\circ - m\\angle B = 180^\\circ - 112^\\circ = 68^\\circ$$</p>\n<p>(b) First check the triangle&#x27;s shape: $6^2 + 8^2 = 36 + 64 = 100 = 10^2$, so by the Converse of the Pythagorean Theorem the triangle is right, with hypotenuse 10 ft. The center of a circumscribed circle (the circumcenter) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides, and it is equidistant from all three vertices. For a right triangle, the midpoint of the hypotenuse is equidistant from all three vertices (it is the circumcenter), so the center is the midpoint of the 10-ft side. The radius is half the hypotenuse:<br>$$r = \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ ft}$$</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) The remaining tower angles are $m\\angle C = 95^\\circ$ and $m\\angle D = 68^\\circ$. (b) The ring&#x27;s center is the midpoint of the sign&#x27;s 10-ft hypotenuse, and its radius is 5 feet.</p>",
 "41": "<p>(a) $120^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ radians.</p>\n<p>(b) Arc length: $s = r\\theta = 12 \\times \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} = 8\\pi \\approx 25.13$ m.</p>\n<p>(c) Sector area: $A = \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}(12^2)\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}(144)\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = 48\\pi \\approx 150.80$ m².</p>\n<p>(d) The sector is the fraction $\\dfrac{\\theta}{2\\pi}$ of the full circle; with $\\theta = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ this fraction is $\\frac{1}{3}$, and indeed $\\frac{1}{3}\\pi(12)^2 = 48\\pi$ m². The formula $A = \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta$ works because $\\theta$ in radians already measures the sector as a proportion of the full $2\\pi$-radian circle.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sprinkler rotates through $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ radians; the outer arc is $8\\pi \\approx 25.13$ meters long, and the watered area is $48\\pi \\approx 150.80$ square meters (one-third of the full circle of radius 12 m).</p>",
 "42": "<p>(a) $105^\\circ \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}$ radians ($\\approx 1.8326$ rad).</p>\n<p>(b) Arc length: $s = r\\theta = 28 \\times \\dfrac{7\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{49\\pi}{3} \\approx 51.3$ inches.</p>\n<p>(c) Sector area: $A = \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}(28^2)\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}(784)\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\frac{686\\pi}{3} \\approx 718.4$ square inches.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The wiper sweeps through $\\frac{7\\pi}{12}$ radians; its tip traces an arc of about 51.3 inches, and one sweep cleans about 718.4 square inches of windshield.</p>",
 "43": "<p>(a) Group the $x$-terms and $y$-terms and complete the square:<br>$$x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 4y = 12$$<br>$$(x^2 - 6x + 9) + (y^2 + 4y + 4) = 12 + 9 + 4$$<br>$$(x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25$$<br>Center: $(3, -2)$; radius: $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ miles.</p>\n<p>(b) Distance from the center $(3, -2)$ to the bus stop $(7, 1)$:<br>$$d = \\sqrt{(7-3)^2 + (1-(-2))^2} = \\sqrt{16 + 9} = \\sqrt{25} = 5 \\text{ miles}$$<br>The distance equals the radius exactly.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The coverage circle is $(x-3)^2 + (y+2)^2 = 25$, centered at $(3, -2)$ with radius 5 miles; the bus stop at $(7,1)$ lies exactly <strong>on</strong> the coverage boundary.</p>",
 "44": "<p>(a) A point $(x, y)$ is on the boundary exactly when its distance from the center $(-2, 4)$ is 7 m. By the Distance Formula:<br>$$\\sqrt{(x - (-2))^2 + (y - 4)^2} = 7$$<br>Squaring both sides:<br>$$(x + 2)^2 + (y - 4)^2 = 49$$</p>\n<p>(b) Substitute the gate $(5, 4)$: $(5 + 2)^2 + (4 - 4)^2 = 7^2 + 0 = 49$. ✓ The equation is satisfied, so the gate lies exactly on the boundary (7 m due east of the center).</p>\n<p>(c) Distance from the center $(-2, 4)$ to the bench $(-2, 12)$:<br>$$d = \\sqrt{(-2-(-2))^2 + (12-4)^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 64} = 8 \\text{ m}$$<br>Since $8 &gt; 7$, the bench is farther from the center than the radius.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The boundary is $(x+2)^2 + (y-4)^2 = 49$; the gate at $(5,4)$ is exactly on the boundary, and the bench at $(-2,12)$ is outside the park, 8 m from the center.</p>",
 "45": "<p>(a) Let $P = (x, y)$ be any point on the parabola. Its distance to the focus $(0, 2)$ is $\\sqrt{x^2 + (y-2)^2}$, and its distance to the directrix $y = -2$ is $|y - (-2)| = y + 2$ (for points on the dish, $y \\ge 0$, so this is positive). By the definition of a parabola, set the distances equal:<br>$$\\sqrt{x^2 + (y-2)^2} = y + 2$$<br>Square both sides:<br>$$x^2 + (y-2)^2 = (y+2)^2$$<br>$$x^2 + y^2 - 4y + 4 = y^2 + 4y + 4$$<br>Cancel $y^2$ and 4 from both sides:<br>$$x^2 - 4y = 4y \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^2 = 8y$$</p>\n<p>(b) At the rim height $y = 8$: $x^2 = 8(8) = 64$, so $x = \\pm 8$. The rim extends from $x = -8$ to $x = 8$, a width of $8 - (-8) = 16$ inches.</p>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> The cross-section is $x^2 = 8y$ (equivalently $y = \\frac{x^2}{8}$), with the receiver at the focus $(0, 2)$; at a height of 8 inches the dish is 16 inches wide.</p>\n<p>---</p>",
 "46": "<p>The barrel is a cylinder. The diameter is 24 in., so the radius is r = 12 in., and h = 36 in.</p>\n<p>V = πr²h = π(12)²(36) = 5,184π ≈ 16,286.0 in³</p>\n<p>Convert to gallons: 16,286.0 ÷ 231 ≈ 70.5 gallons.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The full barrel holds about 70.5 gallons of water.</strong></p>",
 "47": "<p>The pile is a cone with r = 6 ft and h = 4 ft.</p>\n<p>V = (1/3)πr²h = (1/3)π(6)²(4) = 48π ≈ 150.8 ft³</p>\n<p>Convert to cubic yards: 150.8 ÷ 27 ≈ 5.59 yd³.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The pile contains about 150.8 cubic feet, or about 5.6 cubic yards of mulch.</strong></p>",
 "48": "<p><strong>Right circular cylinder:</strong><br>(a) Parallel to the base: a <strong>circle</strong>, the same size as the base.<br>(b) Perpendicular to the base through the central axis: a <strong>rectangle</strong>, with width equal to the diameter and height equal to the cylinder&#x27;s height.<br>(c) Slanted cut: an <strong>ellipse</strong> (an oval), because the cutting plane slices the circular wall at an angle.</p>\n<p><strong>Square pyramid:</strong><br>(a) Parallel to the base: a <strong>square</strong>, smaller than the base and similar to it.<br>(b) Perpendicular to the base through the apex and the center of the base: a <strong>triangle</strong>.<br>(c) Slanted cut that does not pass through the apex: a <strong>trapezoid</strong> (a quadrilateral with one pair of parallel sides), because the tilted plane still meets the pairs of opposite faces unequally.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Cylinder — circle, rectangle, ellipse. Square pyramid — square, triangle, trapezoid.</strong></p>",
 "49": "<p><strong>Profile 1 (rectangle):</strong> Rotating a rectangle about one of its sides generates a <strong>cylinder</strong>. The side on the axis becomes the height, h = 6 in., and the width becomes the radius, r = 4 in.</p>\n<p>V = πr²h = π(4)²(6) = 96π ≈ 301.6 in³</p>\n<p><strong>Profile 2 (right triangle):</strong> Rotating a right triangle about one of its legs generates a <strong>cone</strong>. The leg on the axis becomes the height, h = 6 in., and the other leg becomes the radius, r = 4 in.</p>\n<p>V = (1/3)πr²h = (1/3)π(4)²(6) = 32π ≈ 100.5 in³</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The rectangle generates a cylinder with r = 4 in., h = 6 in., and volume ≈ 301.6 in³; the triangle generates a cone with r = 4 in., h = 6 in., and volume ≈ 100.5 in³ — exactly one-third of the cylinder&#x27;s volume, as expected for a cone and cylinder with the same base and height.</strong></p>",
 "50": "<p>Rectangle: A = 30 × 20 = 600 ft².<br>Semicircle: the diameter is 20 ft, so r = 10 ft, and A = (1/2)πr² = (1/2)π(10)² = 50π ≈ 157.1 ft².</p>\n<p>Total area = 600 + 157.1 = 757.1 ft².</p>\n<p>Boxes needed: 757.1 ÷ 10 = 75.71 boxes. Since boxes are sold whole and the area must be fully covered, round <strong>up</strong> to 76 boxes.</p>\n<p>Assumption: this count allows no extra material for cutting waste or breakage; in practice the school might order one or two additional boxes as a cushion.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: About 757.1 square feet must be resurfaced, requiring 76 boxes of pavers (assuming no allowance for waste).</strong></p>",
 "51": "<p>Volume of the pad: V = 4 × 3 × 0.15 = 1.8 m³.</p>\n<p>Mass = density × volume = 2,400 kg/m³ × 1.8 m³ = 4,320 kg.</p>\n<p>Compare with the trailer rating: 4,320 kg &lt; 5,000 kg, so the mass is within the trailer&#x27;s capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The pad&#x27;s concrete has a mass of 4,320 kilograms, which is under the 5,000-kilogram limit, so it can be hauled in a single trailer load by mass.</strong></p>",
 "52": "<p>(a) Density = population ÷ area = 18,400 ÷ 8.5 ≈ 2,164.7, or about <strong>2,165 people per square mile</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) After annexation: population = 18,400 + 1,200 = 19,600 people; area = 8.5 + 0.4 = 8.9 mi².<br>Density = 19,600 ÷ 8.9 ≈ 2,202.2, or about <strong>2,202 people per square mile</strong>. (Note: both the numerator and denominator must be updated; averaging the two densities would be incorrect.)</p>\n<p>(c) 2,202 &lt; 2,250, so the town stays within the guideline.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Current density ≈ 2,165 people/mi²; after annexation ≈ 2,202 people/mi², which is still within the 2,250 people/mi² guideline.</strong></p>",
 "53": "<p>For each radius, the height follows from the fixed volume: V = πr²h = 355, so h = 355 ÷ (πr²). The closed-can surface area is S = 2πr² + 2πrh.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Radius r (cm)</th><th>Height h = 355/(πr²) (cm)</th><th>Surface area S (cm²)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>≈ 12.56</td><td>≈ 293.2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>≈ 7.06</td><td>≈ 278.0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>≈ 4.52</td><td>≈ 299.1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>≈ 3.14</td><td>≈ 344.5</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Sample computation for r = 4: h = 355 ÷ (16π) ≈ 7.06 cm; S = 2π(16) + 2π(4)(7.06) ≈ 100.5 + 177.5 ≈ 278.0 cm².</p>\n<p>The smallest surface area among the tested radii occurs at r = 4 cm.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Of the designs tested, the can with radius 4 cm and height about 7.06 cm uses the least aluminum, with a total surface area of about 278.0 cm².</strong></p>",
 "54": "<p><strong>Design A (rectangular prism, 20 ft × 10 ft × 5 ft):</strong><br>• Volume: V = 20 × 10 × 5 = 1,000 ft³ → excavation cost = 1,000 × $8 = $8,000.<br>• Interior surface: bottom = 20 × 10 = 200 ft²; side walls = 2(20 × 5) + 2(10 × 5) = 200 + 100 = 300 ft²; total = 500 ft² → coating cost = 500 × $3 = $1,500.<br>• Total cost: $8,000 + $1,500 = <strong>$9,500</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Design B (cylinder, r = 8 ft, depth 5 ft):</strong><br>• Volume: V = π(8)²(5) = 320π ≈ 1,005.3 ft³ → excavation cost ≈ 1,005.3 × $8 ≈ $8,042.48.<br>• Interior surface: bottom = π(8)² ≈ 201.1 ft²; side wall = 2π(8)(5) ≈ 251.3 ft²; total ≈ 452.4 ft² → coating cost ≈ 452.4 × $3 ≈ $1,357.17.<br>• Total cost: ≈ $8,042.48 + $1,357.17 ≈ <strong>$9,399.65</strong>.</p>\n<p>Comparison: $9,500 − $9,399.65 ≈ $100.35. Design B holds slightly more water but its smaller interior surface area more than offsets the extra excavation.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Design A costs $9,500 and Design B costs about $9,399.65, so the center should choose Design B, saving about $100.35.</strong></p>",
 "55": "<p>(a) <strong>Sample survey.</strong> Population: all 12,000 students in the district. The district wants a percentage describing the whole student body, so it should survey a random sample of students and generalize — no treatment is being applied and no cause is being claimed.</p>\n<p>(b) <strong>Experiment.</strong> Population: the district&#x27;s algebra students. The question asks whether the program <em>causes</em> higher scores, and only an experiment — with students assigned (ideally randomly) to the tutoring program or to a comparison condition — can support a cause-and-effect conclusion.</p>\n<p>(c) <strong>Observational study.</strong> Population: the district&#x27;s current students. Because the district will not (and ethically could not) assign sleep schedules, researchers can only observe existing sleep habits and GPAs; the study can show an association but cannot prove that sleep causes the GPA difference, since other variables (e.g., course load, jobs) may be involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: (a) sample survey of the district&#x27;s 12,000 students; (b) experiment on the district&#x27;s algebra students; (c) observational study of the district&#x27;s current students.</strong></p>",
 "56": "<p>(i) <strong>Sample survey.</strong> Randomization: random <strong>selection</strong> of addresses. Random selection makes the sample representative, so results can be generalized to all county residents. No treatment is assigned, so no causal claim is involved.</p>\n<p>(ii) <strong>Experiment.</strong> Randomization: random <strong>assignment</strong> of volunteers to the two therapies. Random assignment balances the groups on other variables, so a difference in pain scores can be attributed to the therapy — a cause-and-effect conclusion is justified (for people like the volunteers). Note there is no random selection from a larger population, so generalizing beyond similar patients requires care.</p>\n<p>(iii) <strong>Observational study.</strong> Randomization: <strong>neither</strong> — the researcher observes existing groups and assigns nothing. The study can show an association between gym membership and heart rate, but it cannot establish that gym membership <em>causes</em> lower heart rates, because gym members may differ in diet, age, or other habits (confounding).</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: (i) Sample survey — random selection supports generalizing to the county. (ii) Experiment — random assignment supports a causal conclusion. (iii) Observational study — no randomization, so only association can be claimed.</strong></p>",
 "57": "<p>(a) Sample proportion: p̂ = 124 ÷ 200 = <strong>0.62</strong>, i.e., 62% of the sample supports the program.</p>\n<p>(b) Margin of error ≈ 2 × (standard deviation of the simulated sample proportions) = 2 × 0.034 = <strong>0.068</strong>, or 6.8 percentage points.<br>Interval: 0.62 − 0.068 = 0.552 to 0.62 + 0.068 = 0.688, i.e., <strong>55.2% to 68.8%</strong>.</p>\n<p>(c) Interpretation: based on the random sample, the percent of all 1,800 students who support the program is plausibly between about 55.2% and 68.8%. Because the entire interval lies above 50%, the council can reasonably claim that a majority of all students support the program — even the lowest plausible value is still a majority.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: p̂ = 0.62; margin of error ≈ 0.068; interval ≈ 55.2% to 68.8%. Yes, a majority claim is reasonable because the whole interval exceeds 50%.</strong></p>",
 "58": "<p>(a) Margin of error ≈ 2 × 2.3 = <strong>4.6 minutes</strong>.<br>Interval: 28.4 − 4.6 = 23.8 to 28.4 + 4.6 = 33.0, i.e., <strong>23.8 to 33.0 minutes</strong>.</p>\n<p>(b) Yes. The entire interval lies below 35 minutes — even the highest plausible value for the city&#x27;s mean commute (33.0 minutes) is under 35 — so the interval supports the claim that the mean commute is below 35 minutes.</p>\n<p>(c) Assumption: the 40 workers were randomly selected and are representative of all city workers (e.g., the sample was not limited to one neighborhood, employer, or shift), so the sample mean can be generalized to the city.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: Margin of error ≈ 4.6 minutes; interval ≈ 23.8 to 33.0 minutes, which supports the under-35-minutes claim; the key assumption is a random, representative sample of all city workers.</strong></p>",
 "59": "<p>(a) Proportion at least as large by chance: 18 ÷ 1,000 = <strong>0.018</strong>, or 1.8%.</p>\n<p>(b) 1.8% &lt; 5%, so yes — the observed difference is <strong>statistically significant</strong> at the 5% guideline. (Consistent check: 4.3 points is about 2.8 simulated standard deviations from 0, well outside the usual range of the shuffled differences.)</p>\n<p>(c) Conclusion: the data provide convincing evidence that App A produces greater average improvement than App B for students like these. Random assignment matters because it balanced the two groups on motivation, prior knowledge, and other variables, so the 4.3-point gap can be attributed to the apps themselves rather than to pre-existing differences between the groups — a difference this large would arise from the random assignment alone only about 1.8% of the time.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: 1.8% of simulations matched or beat the observed difference; the result is statistically significant, supporting the conclusion that App A is more effective, a causal conclusion justified by the random assignment.</strong></p>",
 "60": "<p>(a) Proportion at least as extreme by chance: 505 ÷ 1,000 = <strong>0.505</strong>, or 50.5%.</p>\n<p>(b) 50.5% is far above 5%, so the observed difference is <strong>not statistically significant</strong>. (Consistent check: 0.06 s is less than one simulated standard deviation, 0.09 s, from 0 — an entirely ordinary result under random assignment.)</p>\n<p>(c) Conclusion for the coach: these data do not provide convincing evidence that Routine X reduces 100-meter times more than Routine Y. A difference of 0.06 second is well within what random assignment alone commonly produces. This does <strong>not</strong> prove the routines are exactly equal — only that this study found no meaningful evidence of a difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: 50.5% of simulations were at least as extreme; the difference is not statistically significant, so the coach should conclude there is no convincing evidence that either routine is better.</strong></p>",
 "61": "<p>Problems with the poll (any four or more, with their effects):<br>1. <strong>Voluntary (self-selected) response:</strong> only people motivated enough to visit the site and click were counted. People with strong feelings — especially opponents — are far more likely to respond, inflating the &quot;oppose&quot; percentage.<br>2. <strong>Wrong sampling frame:</strong> respondents are visitors to a sports-talk radio website, not a cross-section of city residents. Their views cannot be generalized to all residents, which is what the headline claims.<br>3. <strong>Leading question wording:</strong> &quot;wasteful&quot; and &quot;before it raises your taxes&quot; push respondents toward opposing, so the answers reflect the wording as much as residents&#x27; actual opinions.<br>4. <strong>No margin of error reported:</strong> with only 200 respondents, sampling variability alone would be several percentage points, so &quot;90%&quot; carries unstated uncertainty — and the headline presents it as exact.<br>5. <strong>Small, one-weekend snapshot:</strong> 200 self-chosen clicks over a single weekend cannot represent a whole city&#x27;s stable opinion.</p>\n<p>A trustworthy poll would: randomly select a sample of city residents (e.g., from voter or address records) large enough for a small margin of error, ask a neutrally worded question (e.g., &quot;Do you support or oppose building the new stadium?&quot;), report the sample size and margin of error, and describe who was surveyed and when.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: The poll is a self-selected sample of sports-radio website visitors answering a loaded question with no reported margin of error, so it cannot support a claim about &quot;90% of city residents&quot;; a valid poll needs random sampling of residents, neutral wording, and a reported margin of error.</strong></p>",
 "62": "<p>(a) The study is an uncontrolled observational follow-up, not an experiment: no group was assigned to <em>not</em> do the program, so there is no comparison showing what would have happened without it. Weight changes could be due to the diet coaching, seasonal habits, motivation, or other factors. Without random assignment to program vs. control, &quot;causes&quot; is not supported.</p>\n<p>(b) Two additional problems:<br>• <strong>Dropout exclusion (survivorship bias):</strong> the 84 members who quit — plausibly those losing the least weight, or even gaining — were removed, so the 10-pound average describes only the 96 finishers, not the 180 who enrolled. The average result for a typical new member would very likely be lower.<br>• <strong>Confounding by diet coaching:</strong> finishers also received optional diet coaching, so even the finishers&#x27; loss cannot be credited to the exercise program alone.<br>(Also acceptable: members are self-selected and motivated, so results need not generalize to the general public.)</p>\n<p>(c) A study that could support causation: recruit volunteers and <strong>randomly assign</strong> them to either the 12-week program or a control condition (e.g., a waitlist or a general-information session), keep other supports (such as diet coaching) the same for both groups or offer them to neither, measure everyone&#x27;s weight change at 12 weeks <strong>including</strong> those who stop attending, and compare the groups&#x27; average changes. Random assignment balances motivation and other variables, so a resulting difference can be attributed to the program.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: As designed, the claim fails on three counts — no control group or random assignment (so no causation), an average computed only over finishers after dropping 84 quitters, and diet coaching as a confounder. A randomized experiment with a control group, equal conditions, and all participants counted would be needed to support the word &quot;causes.&quot;</strong></p>"
};
